'''İkiztepe''' ({{Langx|ku|Têzxerab}}; {{langx|syr|Tizharab}}){{sfnp|Jongerden|Verheij|2012|p=325}}{{efn|Alternatively transliterated as Tezharap, Tīz Ḫrāb, and Tiz-Harab.{{sfnmp|1a1=Gaunt|1y=2006|1p=425|2a1=Jongerden|2a2=Verheij|2y=2012|2p=325|Bcheiry|2009|3p=60}}}} is a village in the municipality and district of [[Nusaybin]], [[Mardin Province]] in [[Turkey]].<ref>[https://www.e-icisleri.gov.tr/Anasayfa/MulkiIdariBolumleri.aspx Mahalle], Turkey Civil Administration Departments Inventory. Retrieved 19 September 2023.</ref> The village is populated by [[Kurds]] of the [[Kurdish tribes|Bêcirmanî tribe]] and had a population of 347 in 2021.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=31 ARALIK 2021 TARİHLİ ADRESE DAYALI NÜFUS KAYIT SİSTEMİ (ADNKS) SONUÇLARI |url=https://www.tuik.gov.tr/indir/duyuru/favori_raporlar.xlsx |access-date=16 December 2022 |website=TÜİK |language=tr |format=XLS}}</ref>{{sfnp|Tan|2018|p=371}}

==History==
Tizharab (today called İkiztepe) was historically inhabited by [[Syriac Orthodox Church|Syriac Orthodox Christians]].{{sfnp|Jongerden|Verheij|2012|p=325}} In the Syriac Orthodox patriarchal register of dues of 1870, it was recorded that the village had ten households, who paid sixty-three dues, and it did not have a church or a priest.{{sfnp|Bcheiry|2009|p=60}} The village was inhabited by 300 [[Assyrian people|Syriacs]] in 1914, according to the Assyro-Chaldean delegation to the [[Paris Peace Conference (1919–1920)|Paris Peace Conference]].{{sfnp|Gaunt|2006|p=425}}

